# See .github/workflows/build.yml's Trivy scan job (trivyignores input).
#
# Every entry here is investigated and justified individually -- this file
# is for genuinely unreachable findings only, never a blanket suppression.
# Re-check each entry whenever the base image or requirements.txt changes;
# don't just keep adding to it.

# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 -- msgpack 1.1.2, HIGH.
# This is pip's own vendored copy (site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack),
# bundled inside the base image's pip installation for pip's *own*
# internal HTTP-cache serialization. This app's code never imports
# msgpack, and pip itself is only invoked at build time (`pip install -r
# requirements.txt` in the Dockerfile) -- it is not present in the running
# process, never receives network/user input at runtime, and ships in the
# image purely as a byproduct of how pip is packaged.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# Note: an earlier round of this file also carried a per-CVE-ID entry for
# a Trivy false positive against a phantom "setuptools 70.3.0" -- Trivy
# was misparsing pip/_vendor/vendor.txt (a plain-text manifest, not an
# installed package) as setuptools being installed. That kept recurring
# under a fresh CVE ID every time a new setuptools CVE got published
# (CVE-2025-47273, then CVE-2026-59890 turned up by the very next scan),
# which a per-ID ignore list can't keep up with. Fixed at the root instead
# -- see the Dockerfile, which now deletes that file at build time (pip
# doesn't read it; confirmed pip install/import both still work without
# it) -- so no ignore entry should be needed for it going forward. If a
# new "setuptools" or other pip/_vendor/vendor.txt-sourced finding shows
# up again here, check whether the Dockerfile's rm actually ran before
# reaching for a new ignore entry.
